{"case_id":"seeded-werkzeug-password-compare","kind":"seeded","sample":0,"findings":[{"id":"F1","title":"Use of non-constant-time comparison for password hash","category":"security","severity":"high","confidence":"high","anchor":{"path":"src/werkzeug/security.py","side":"RIGHT","start_line":146,"end_line":146},"explanation":"Replacing hmac.compare_digest with a standard string comparison (==) introduces a timing attack vulnerability. Standard string comparisons short-circuit upon finding the first differing character, allowing an attacker to measure comparison times and potentially deduce password hashes character by character.","suggested_fix":"Restore the use of hmac.compare_digest for comparing the password hashes.","anchor_in_diff":true,"evidence":[]}],"matches":[{"finding_id":"F1","issue_id":"timing","distance":0,"verdict":"match","rationale":"location-only matching"}],"expected_total":1,"decoy_hits":[],"review_usage":{"input_tokens":999,"output_tokens":214,"thinking_tokens":0,"cached_input_tokens":0},"judge_usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0,"thinking_tokens":0,"cached_input_tokens":0},"cached":false,"requests":1,"cached_requests":0,"agent":null,"duration_seconds":12.692,"error":null}
{"case_id":"seeded-werkzeug-password-compare","kind":"seeded","sample":1,"findings":[{"id":"F1","title":"Use of non-constant-time string comparison for password verification","category":"security","severity":"high","confidence":"high","anchor":{"path":"src/werkzeug/security.py","side":"RIGHT","start_line":146,"end_line":146},"explanation":"Replacing hmac.compare_digest with the standard equality operator (==) introduces a timing attack vulnerability. Standard string comparisons short-circuit upon finding the first mismatching character, allowing an attacker to measure response times to incrementally guess password hashes.","suggested_fix":"Restore hmac.compare_digest(_hash_internal(method, salt, password)[0], hashval)","anchor_in_diff":true,"evidence":[]}],"matches":[{"finding_id":"F1","issue_id":"timing","distance":0,"verdict":"match","rationale":"location-only matching"}],"expected_total":1,"decoy_hits":[],"review_usage":{"input_tokens":999,"output_tokens":227,"thinking_tokens":0,"cached_input_tokens":0},"judge_usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0,"thinking_tokens":0,"cached_input_tokens":0},"cached":false,"requests":1,"cached_requests":0,"agent":null,"duration_seconds":8.218,"error":null}
{"case_id":"seeded-werkzeug-proxy-fix-client-ip","kind":"seeded","sample":0,"findings":[{"id":"F1","title":"IPv6 addresses without ports in X-Forwarded-Host will be incorrectly split","category":"correctness","severity":"medium","confidence":"high","anchor":{"path":"src/werkzeug/middleware/proxy_fix.py","side":"RIGHT","start_line":164,"end_line":165},"explanation":"Removing the check for `x_host.endswith(']')` means an IPv6 address without a port (e.g., `[2001:db8::1]`) will be split at the last colon by `rsplit(':', 1)`, resulting in `SERVER_NAME` becoming `[2001:db8` and `SERVER_PORT` becoming `1]`. This breaks handling of IPv6 host headers.","suggested_fix":"Restore the check for IPv6 addresses without ports or use proper address parsing.","anchor_in_diff":true,"evidence":[]}],"matches":[{"finding_id":"F1","issue_id":"ipv6-host","distance":0,"verdict":"match","rationale":"location-only matching"}],"expected_total":2,"decoy_hits":[],"review_usage":{"input_tokens":1479,"output_tokens":294,"thinking_tokens":0,"cached_input_tokens":0},"judge_usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0,"thinking_tokens":0,"cached_input_tokens":0},"cached":false,"requests":1,"cached_requests":0,"agent":null,"duration_seconds":7.691,"error":null}
{"case_id":"seeded-werkzeug-proxy-fix-client-ip","kind":"seeded","sample":1,"findings":[{"id":"F1","title":"IPv6 addresses without port in X-Forwarded-Host will be incorrectly split","category":"correctness","severity":"high","confidence":"high","anchor":{"path":"src/werkzeug/middleware/proxy_fix.py","side":"RIGHT","start_line":164,"end_line":165},"explanation":"Removing the check `and not x_host.endswith(\"]\")` means an IPv6 address without a port (e.g., `[2001:db8::1]`) will be split on the last colon by `rsplit(\":\", 1)`, corrupting `SERVER_NAME` into `[2001:db8:` and `SERVER_PORT` into `1]`. The check was necessary to distinguish between an IPv6 address without a port and an IPv6 address with a port.","suggested_fix":"Restore the check for IPv6 addresses ending with ']' before splitting.","anchor_in_diff":true,"evidence":[]}],"matches":[{"finding_id":"F1","issue_id":"ipv6-host","distance":0,"verdict":"match","rationale":"location-only matching"}],"expected_total":2,"decoy_hits":[],"review_usage":{"input_tokens":1479,"output_tokens":301,"thinking_tokens":0,"cached_input_tokens":0},"judge_usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0,"thinking_tokens":0,"cached_input_tokens":0},"cached":false,"requests":1,"cached_requests":0,"agent":null,"duration_seconds":7.194,"error":null}
